# Freestanding FABRICATED-STUB baseline -- NEW-ONLY RATCHET
#
# Format: one `<entry-key> <symbol>` per line. `#` starts a comment.
# `<entry-key>` is the output binary's basename (see `fabricated_stub_entry_key`
# in src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/pipeline/native_project/stubs.rs, which is
# deliberately identical to `simpleos_entry_key` in
# src/compiler/70.backend/backend/llvm_native_link.spl).
#
# WHAT THIS GATES
#   `generate_stub_object_freestanding` emits, for every unresolved symbol in a
#   freestanding link when SIMPLE_ALLOW_FREESTANDING_STUBS=1:
#
#       __attribute__((weak)) __stub_i64 __stub_fs_N(void) __asm__("<sym>");
#       __attribute__((weak)) __stub_i64 __stub_fs_N(void) { return 0; }
#
#   That is FABRICATION: the link succeeds and every caller silently receives
#   nil. This mechanism shipped a nil-returning `rt_array_copy` into a guest and
#   shredded every array copy, with the wrong answer surfacing nine steps
#   downstream. The guard runs at that emission site, in the Rust linker
#   pipeline, which is the only freestanding link path that actually executes
#   (`--target x86_64-unknown-none` links via `clang --target=x86_64-unknown-elf`
#   from linker.rs; no `.spl` link path is involved).
#
# NO CLASSIFIER, NO FALSE POSITIVES
#   This is not the sibling file `config/simpleos_fabricated_rt_baseline.sdn`,
#   which classifies BODIES from objdump output and therefore needs careful
#   weak-vs-trivial reasoning. Here the compiler reports exactly the symbols it
#   is itself about to fabricate, so a row can never be a misclassification.
#   Do not merge the two files: they measure different sets on different
#   channels, and a shared file would reintroduce exactly the doc-vs-code drift
#   that hid the original defect.
#
# SEMANTICS, enforced by `check_fabricated_stub_ratchet`:
#   * Every fabricated symbol is PRINTED, named, on every build -- as
#     `FABRICATED <entry> <sym>` (baselined) or `FABRICATED-NEW <entry> <sym>`.
#     Reporting never depends on this file, so deleting the file cannot make
#     fabrication invisible.
#   * The link FAILS when an entry that HAS rows here fabricates a symbol that
#     is not among them. NEW fabrication is the thing this gate catches.
#   * An entry with NO rows here is UNBASELINED: it reports every symbol and
#     prints a WARNING, but does not fail -- the production freestanding link
#     currently cannot link without these stubs, so a big-bang failure would
#     take the gate offline instead of ratcheting it. Set
#     SIMPLE_STRICT_FABRICATED_STUB_RATCHET=1 to turn that warning into a
#     failure (the end state, once the implementations exist).
#   * A malformed row is a hard error, never a silently empty baseline.
#
# THIS IS NOT AN ALLOWLIST. Rows are dated debt, not a claim that nil is
# correct. Never add a row to make a new failure go away: implement the symbol
# instead. Every remediation wave DELETES rows here in the same commit that
# lands the implementation.
#
# REGENERATION -- this procedure IS what the code does, by construction.
#   Re-run the exact build you are baselining with
#
#       SIMPLE_FABRICATED_STUB_BASELINE_WRITE=1
#
#   set in addition to its normal environment (including
#   SIMPLE_ALLOW_FREESTANDING_STUBS=1). The compiler then replaces this file's
#   rows FOR THAT ENTRY KEY ONLY with the set it just fabricated, preserving
#   every comment and every other entry, and does not fail the link. There is no
#   separate hand-run tool and no second procedure to drift from -- the writer
#   and the checker read the same set from the same variable.
#
#   Override the file location with SIMPLE_FABRICATED_STUB_BASELINE=<path>
#   (used by tests; not for production builds).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rows below this line. Entry keys seen so far are recorded by the procedure
# above; an entry absent here has never been measured (see UNBASELINED, above).
